.he "cal (1)                      9/30/26                      cal (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
cal
- print a calendar
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
cal [ month ] [ year ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Cal prints a calendar: a whole year, or a single month of one.  A
month may be spelled out, abbreviated, or given as a number, and a
year may be left off to mean this one.
.sp
With no argument at all, cal prints a whole-year calendar for the
year the clock is set to.
.sp
With one argument, cal prints a whole-year calendar for that year.
An argument that is not a number is read as a month instead, and then
cal prints that month of the current year.
.sp
With two arguments the first is the month and the second is the
year, so cal 8 2026 is August 2026.  Where both arguments are numbers
the first is taken to be the month.
.sp
A month name is matched without regard to case and by its first
letters, so august, August and aug are all August.  A short
abbreviation can be ambiguous, and the first month it fits wins:
january, jun and jul all begin with ju, so ju is June, and ma is
March.
.sp
Years run from 1, there being no year 0, to 9999; the calendar
changes from the Julian to the Gregorian reckoning in September 1752,
and cal prints that month with its eighteen days.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLES
.in +5
.br
.in +5
.br
.bd
cal
.sp
Print this year's calendar.
.sp
.bd
cal 1932
.sp
Print a whole-year calendar for 1932.
.sp
.bd
cal august 1955
.sp
Print a single-month calendar for August, 1955.
.sp
.bd
cal november
.sp
Print November of this year.

.in -5
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The recovered cal (1) of July, 1982 does not say what cal does with
no argument, and gives no year range; both are settled above.  The
year is read from the clock with time and localtime, so a machine
whose clock is wrong prints the wrong calendar.
.sp
The /bin/cal on the 1.6 disk does not read the year argument at all.
There, cal 1932 prints this year's calendar and not 1932's, so that
binary does not match its own page and this command is what does.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
date (1), pr (1)
